window.docContents[215].resolve({"/#Jakarta-Persistence--3___-Entity-Operations--3___6___-Entity-Instance-Lifecycle--3___6___3___-Persisting-an-Entity-Instance":{"contents":"A new instance becomes persistent when it is passed as an argument to the persist() method of EntityManager or when the persist operation cascades from another instance to the new instance.\n\nLet e be an instance of an entity class. If e is passed as an argument to the persist() method of EntityManager, or if the persist operation is applied to e via cascade:\n\n* if e is a new entity, it becomes managed and a new record represented by e will be inserted into the database at or before transaction commit as a result of the flush operation specified below in Section 3.6.6;* if e is a managed entity already associated with the persistence context, e itself is ignored by the persist operation;* if e is a removed entity, it becomes managed; or* if e is a detached entity, either an EntityExistsException is thrown immediately, or an EntityExistsException or some other subtype of PersistenceException is thrown when the persistence context is flushed or when the transaction commits.* If e is new, managed, or removed, the persist operation is also applied to every entity t referenced by a relationship owned by e and annotated with the cascade element value cascade=PERSIST or cascade=ALL or mapped as such by the equivalent XML descriptor element.\n\nOrdinarily, the persist operation assigns a persistent identity to the entity. If the primary key of the entity is a generated value, as defined by Section 11.2.23, the primary key is generated as part of the persist operation, and the generated value is made available to the application by setting the primary key field or property of the entity. As a special exception to this rule, if the primary key generation strategy of an entity is IDENTITY, the persistence provider is permitted to delay assignment of the primary key until pending modifications are synchronized with the database, as defined by Section 3.6.6. Indeed, when persist() is called with no active transaction, the persistence provider is required to delay generation and assignment of IDENTITY primary keys.\n\n\n\nFormalized SpecificationPersist requires a created instance whose lifecycle\nstate is new, managed, or removed. The two require conditions below\nexclude instances that have not been constructed and calls on detached instances.For a new instance, let i : identity :| identityAvailable i chooses an\navailable identity. The symbol :| introduces the condition on that\nchoice. Any identity satisfying it is allowed, and the proofs must cover every\nsuch choice. The action assigns this identity and makes the instance managed.For a removed instance, the action changes its state back to managed, cancelling\nits pending removal. For an already managed instance, neither branch applies,\nso the modeled state remains unchanged.action Persist (e : entity) {\n  require created e\n  require e_phase e ≠ detached\n  if e_phase e = new then\n    let i : identity :| identityAvailable i\n    pid e := i\n    e_phase e := managed\n  else if e_phase e = removed then\n    e_phase e := managed\n}\nIdentity assignment and the change to managed happen in one model step. This\nsimplifies the specification's rules for delayed IDENTITY assignment.\nAssigned identities are never released for reuse in this model. In a finite\nmodel, persisting a new instance becomes impossible when no identity is\navailable. This limit does not represent a JPA exception.\n\n\n\n","context":"Jakarta Persistence\u00093. Entity Operations\u00093.6. Entity Instance Lifecycle","header":"3.6.3. Persisting an Entity Instance","id":"/#Jakarta-Persistence--3___-Entity-Operations--3___6___-Entity-Instance-Lifecycle--3___6___3___-Persisting-an-Entity-Instance"}});